{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the second year after they came out of the land of Egypt, in the first month</b> — From here the Sages said (Sifrei Baha'alotkha 64; Pesahim 6b): there is no earlier and later in the Torah. The reason for this postponement was that when this fourth book came to mention the commandments that Israel were commanded in the wilderness of Sinai at their proper time, it wished to complete the subject of the Tent of Meeting and its arrangement throughout all the days of the wilderness. And it first mentioned the standards and the place of the Tent and the stationing of its attendants and the arrangement of the divisions for its transport for all the service of the Tent, and it mentioned the offerings of the princes who brought the wagons that would carry it all the time they were in the wilderness, and it completed their offerings at the dedication of the altar, which took place from the first of Nisan or earlier. And after that it returned to mention a warning that He had warned them, lest they forget the commandment of Pesah. And according to the opinion of our Sages, it was practiced in the wilderness only in this year alone, because they did not circumcise in the wilderness, and circumcision of males and slaves was a disqualification preventing them. And it is possible that this commandment was required because initially they were not commanded regarding the making of the Pesah of generations except in the land, as it is written: \"And it shall be when you come to the land that HaShem will give you, as He has spoken, and you shall keep this service\" (Exodus 12:25), and it is further said there: \"And it shall be when HaShem brings you into the land of the Canaanite, etc., and you shall perform this service in this month\" (Exodus 13:5). And now the Holy One, blessed be He, wished and commanded that they perform it so that it would be a remembrance of their redemption and the miracles performed for them and for their fathers, handed down to them from the fathers who saw it to their children, and their children to their children, and their children to the last generation. For first He said \"And it shall be when you come to the land\" to say that this commandment does not apply outside the land for generations, and now He commanded that they practice it in the wilderness. And according to the opinion of our Sages they performed it only in this year alone, because they could not perform it since sons and slaves were born to them and they could not circumcise them for the reason they mentioned, for it was a danger to them as is explained in Yevamot (71b). But in Sifrei (Baha'alotkha 67) they said: \"And they performed the Pesah on the first [day],\" etc. — the verse speaks in disparagement of Israel, that they performed only this Pesah alone. And likewise it says: \"Did you bring near to Me sacrifices and meal-offerings in the wilderness forty years?\" (Amos 5:25). They derived this from this verse that says: \"And they performed the Pesah on the first, on the fourteenth day of the month, between the evenings, in the wilderness of Sinai\" (Numbers 9:5), for the verse is superfluous, and it would have been sufficient to say \"And they performed the Pesah according to all that HaShem commanded Moses,\" but it mentioned the day and the wilderness to hint that they did not perform it in the wilderness except on this day, and that is their disparagement. And it is possible that this disparagement refers to their corruption in the matter of the spies, for because of it they were ostracized and the north wind did not blow for them and they did not circumcise, and consequently they were forbidden from all sacrifices and were rebuked. Or perhaps this baraita follows the opinion of the one who says (Mekhilta Bo 15) that circumcision of males and slaves does not disqualify, and they were permitted to perform it but were lazy and did not perform it, and that is a great disparagement. But the first opinion is more correct in my eyes, for Moses would have compelled them to perform it and would not have left them without the obligation of karet. But the Festival of Unleavened Bread for seven days and the cessation of leaven — there was no need to say that they practiced it, for it is a bodily obligation that applies everywhere, and it was already said concerning them: \"An everlasting statute throughout your generations\" (Exodus 12:17)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ס״ד in the Sifrei citation is resolved as 64 based on standard editions; some witnesses read differently."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:00.951403387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}